\vspace{40pt}
{\large\bfseries Application for Growth Product Manager, ApprovalMax}
\vspace{30pt}

Berlin, Germany, 12.06.2026

\vspace{25pt}
Dear Hiring Team,

\vspace{10pt}

The challenge at ApprovalMax is precise and I have solved its direct equivalent before: users register with clear intent, then stall at the step that requires them to connect an external system. At HELLA, that step was connecting a workshop account to place a first digital order. At ApprovalMax, it is connecting Xero or QuickBooks Online. The friction is structurally identical: trust, setup effort, and unclear immediate value. And it sits between registration and the moment where conversion probability roughly doubles.
\vspace{10pt}

At HELLA I owned that funnel end-to-end. I defined what activation meant, worked with engineering to instrument the events, diagnosed the drop-off through cohort and funnel analysis, and shipped in-product fixes through Userlane and checkout redesign. Self-service adoption increased by 40\% and order completion time dropped by 35\%. I did not outsource the diagnosis or the instrumentation; I built it and owned the number.
\vspace{10pt}

I understand the constraint your environment imposes. Fewer than 100 Free Trials per week means classical A/B significance is slow, sometimes unavailable. At Accenture I ran the same kind of iterative experimentation on Natura's checkout funnel, shipping weekly improvements with Amplitude cohort analysis as the primary diagnostic tool rather than waiting for significance thresholds that B2B volumes cannot reach. The answer is faster cycle times, tighter hypotheses, and segment-level analysis---not fewer experiments.
\vspace{10pt}

I use Claude Code, Cursor, and LLM workflows as standard operating procedure for experiment design, instrumentation review, and analysis acceleration. I can produce low-fidelity Figma mockups to unblock experiment variants without a designer dependency, and I understand that the role is about doing, not presenting.

\vspace{15pt}
Kind regards,

\vspace{1pt}
Rodrigo Lopes
